# Executable-format thresholds, one per host.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS.
#
# The format linter has to know which migrations it is allowed to judge. Every
# migration written before this format existed is frozen history — no project in
# the fleet has one pending, and fresh setup installs a snapshot rather than
# replaying (ADR-0036) — so judging them would report violations nobody will
# ever fix.
#
# The boundary is the migration's ID, read from its FILENAME. A filename cannot
# be forgotten the way a frontmatter field can, which is the whole reason the ID
# was chosen over a per-file declaration. A migration MAY additionally declare
# `migration_format: executable` in frontmatter; that can only ADD it to the
# linter's scope, never remove it. The asymmetry is deliberate — opting in is
# always allowed, opting out is not expressible.
#
# WHY IT LIVES IN CORE AND NOT IN THE HOSTS.
#
# Because the linter and its CI step ship here, and a spec that mandates a
# declaration no host has yet written would be false on the day it shipped. Core
# already declares fleet-wide facts this way — see FLEET, ARTIFACTS and
# SHIMMED-HOOKS under reference-implementations/project-hooks/. When the
# installer lands, a host MAY declare its own threshold and take precedence;
# until then this file is the answer.
#
# Each threshold is the next unused ID in that host's migrations/ directory at
# the time this format was adopted. Everything from that number onward MUST
# satisfy the executable form.
#
# RAISING A THRESHOLD IS A RECORDED DECISION, not a convenience. It excuses a
# migration from the format, which is the one thing that lets a silent no-op
# back in. Lowering one is free.
#
# Format: <host-repo-name> <threshold-id>. Blank lines and # comments ignored.

claude-workflow            0035
codex-workflow             0016
opencode-workflow          0012
pi-agentic-apps-workflow   0011
